    notes: "Pass-through ONLY. Pins 1/2/3 of the XLR5 are used purely as three wires \u2014 the headset meanings of those pins (mic ground, mic, earphone return) do not apply. This is NOT a headset or beltpack adapter: plugging one end of a 1-1/2-2/3-3 adapter into a 5-pin headset puts your XLR3 cold on the earphone return and feeds nothing to the earphones. For a headset or a beltpack use the two builds below. Works for mic, line and the Clear-Com/RTS party line (which carries 30 VDC on pin 2 \u2014 a straight wire passes it fine). On a headset-extension cable pins 2 and 3 ride in different shielded pairs (Clear-Com puts 1/2 and 3/4 on separate pairs), so the run is a wire-for-wire pass with reduced hum rejection, not a true balanced pair \u2014 for AES3 use it only if the run is 110\u2013120 \u03a9 DMX-type cable, where pins 2/3 are one pair. Genders: build the two adapters as opposite genders (one female XLR3 \u2192 male XLR5, one female XLR5 \u2192 male XLR3) or they will not mate with the 5-pin run. If the run is carrying a party line OR a phantom-powered mic line, the energised 5-pin end looks exactly like a headset jack or a DMX port: a 5-pin headset or DMX device plugged into it gets 30 V DC (party line) or 48 V phantom on pins 2/3 on its mic / data pins. Turn phantom off before plugging or unplugging the 5-pin run. Label both adapters and never leave a powered run with an open 5-pin end. HelixNet 3-pin ports are worse than a plain party line: +30 V on pin 2 AND \u221230 V on pin 3 (60 V between pins 2 and 3). A 5-pin headset or DMX receiver on an energised run sees that too. Face/solder: the app draws the FEMALE mating face. For a MALE cable end that drawing is exactly what the male insert's solder cups look like, so wire the male straight from the FACE VIEW. For a FEMALE cable end use the app's SOLDER SIDE view (the mirror). Ring both adapters out end to end (1-1, 2-2, 3-3) before the run is ever energised.",
