    notes: "StudioHub+ balanced layout: Hot on pin 1 (Wht/Org), Cold on pin 2 (Orange); second channel Hot 3 (Wht/Grn), Cold 6 (Green). In the official StudioHub+ adapter XLR pin 1 goes to the CABLE SHIELD (shielded Cat5 and shielded RJ45), not to a numbered pin; RJ45 pin 4 is the DC GND rail and pin 5 is n/c. PinRef lands pin 1 on pins 4 (ch1) and 5 (ch2) so the ground has a path on ordinary unshielded Cat5 \u2014 both ends must use the same convention (pair this with RJ45 \u2192 XLR3). Pins 7/8 carry \u00b115 V DC on powered StudioHub+ panels \u2014 never short them to audio ground, and never plug this into an Ethernet switch or PoE port. Never use this for a party line: XLR pin 2 on a Clear-Com/RTS line is +30 V DC, which this layout puts on RJ45 pin 1 \u2014 straight into a StudioHub+ L+ input if the run is ever patched into a studio plant. Never plug the RJ45 into a HelixNet / intercom port: it is pin-incompatible \u2014 RJ45 1/2 are ground and 3\u20138 carry \u00b130 V (Clear-Com's two published HelixNet tables disagree on which pin is which rail), so ch1's XLR pin 1 sits on a 30 V rail against its grounded pins 2/3 and ch2 sees 60 V between its pins \u2014 across a mic either way. Channel order differs on other makers' Cat5 snakes (Radial: orange pair = ch 4; RAT/ETS: orange = ch 2, blue = ch 1; Whirlwind/Radial put a signal pair on 4/5) \u2014 only pair with the PinRef RJ45\u2192XLR3 build. Against an official StudioHub+ adapter the pin-4 ground has no partner (StudioHub puts pin 1 on the shield), so phantom power will not reach a mic through that mix. This end is the FEMALE XLR3 (source side). If the far end is a mixer input with 48 V phantom on, +48 V arrives on this XLR's pins 2 and 3 (relative to pin 1 / RJ45 4) \u2014 keep phantom off unless this XLR is on a condenser mic. Face/solder: this XLR end is FEMALE \u2014 the app draws the FEMALE face (looking into the holes), which is this end's own front, so its solder cups are the mirror: use the app's SOLDER SIDE view or read the moulded pin numbers.",
