    notes: "Verified against Cisco's own table (1750 Hardware Installation Guide, Table 3): console TxD 3 \u2192 PC RxD (DE-9 2), console RxD 6 \u2192 PC TxD (DE-9 3), GND 4/5 \u2192 5, RTS 1 \u2192 CTS 8, CTS 8 \u2192 RTS 7, DTR 2 \u2192 DSR 6, DSR 7 \u2192 DTR 4. This is NOT the Yost standard, although Cisco borrowed the idea: Yost puts RD on RJ45 pin 3 and TD on pin 6 (the mirror of Cisco), DCD (an input) on pin 2 and DTR (an output) on pin 7 \u2014 the mirror of Cisco's DTR on 2 and DSR on 7 \u2014 on a genuinely Yost-wired device this cable ties TD to TD and passes nothing (swap 3\u21946, 1\u21948, 2\u21947 for Yost). Cisco's current web document 12223 prints a console table that disagrees with the 1750 manual and with itself; the manual wins. The cited pinoutguide table numbers the RJ45 at the ADAPTER jack (after the rollover), so it reads as the mirror of this build. For sports scoring (Daktronics All Sport, OmniSport) this is NOT the cable \u2014 those controllers expose PC-style DE-9 RS-232; see the DE-9 builds. Mis-plug: the RJ45 end fits powered jacks \u2014 any PoE or passive injector puts 24\u201357 V on the PC's serial pins (Mode B on 4/5 vs 7/8 = ground and RTS/DTR; Mode A on 1/2 vs 3/6 = CTS/DSR/RxD/TxD); HelixNet puts \u00b130 V across pins 3\u20138, this cable's 4/5 jumper can bridge the two rails and leaves the PC's RS-232 pins 60 V from its ground; StudioHub+ \u00b115 V on 7/8 \u2014 console cables only into console ports. The DE-9 end is FEMALE. Face/solder: the app draws the MALE DE-9 face. A FEMALE shell's solder cups are laid out exactly like that drawing, so wire a female shell straight from the FACE VIEW (its own front is the mirror, 1\u21945, 2\u21944, 6\u21949, 7\u21948, and the app's SOLDER SIDE view shows a MALE shell's cups). Confirm with the moulded numbers. DE-9 pins 1 (DCD) and 9 (RI) are not connected.",
