    notes: "RJ45 side uses the CISCO console pinning (3 = TxD out of the console port, 4/5 = GND, 6 = RxD) \u2014 the mirror of Yost (Yost: 3 = RD, 6 = TD); for a Yost-wired RJ45 device swap 3 and 6. DB25 pins are named from the DTE (PC/terminal) side per EIA-232: pin 2 = TxD, pin 3 = RxD, pin 7 = GND. As drawn the cable connects a CISCO-pinned RJ45 console port to a DTE DB25 with the Tx/Rx cross already built in (for a Yost DTE-adapter jack swap 3 and 6); if the DB25 end is a DCE (modem), swap the DB25 2/3 connections. Not a manufacturer-specific cable \u2014 Swiss Timing / Omega / Tissot venue gear uses RS-485 on proprietary connectors, NOT DB25 RS-232. Add handshaking pins if the target device requires them. Genders: a DTE device port is male, so this adapter's DB25 is FEMALE for a DTE and MALE for a DCE (the app draws the male face). The app's DB25 face labels are the Tascam audio names (pin 2 \u2018Ch 8 Shield\u2019 etc.) \u2014 switch the Pinouts tab to the RS-232 variant; the wiring descriptions here are the serial names. A male DB25 also fits a PC parallel port \u2014 serial only. Mis-plug: the RJ45 end fits powered jacks \u2014 HelixNet puts \u00b130 V (60 V) across pins 3\u20138, this cable's 4/5 jumper can bridge the two rails and puts the DB25 device's RxD/TxD 60 V from its ground; passive PoE 24\u201357 V on 3/6 or 4/5. StudioHub+ is benign here (its \u00b115 V rides on 7/8, which this cable leaves open). Serial jacks only. Face/solder: a FEMALE (DTE) shell's solder cups are laid out exactly like the drawn male face, so wire it straight from the FACE VIEW (its own front is the mirror: top row 1\u219413, 2\u219412, 3\u219411 \u2026 pin 7 stays centre; bottom row 14\u219425 \u2026); a MALE (DCE) shell solders from the SOLDER SIDE view. Confirm with the moulded numbers.",
