    notes: "Pair plan follows Clear-Com's headset-extension rule: the mic circuit (pins 1/2) on one twisted pair and the earphone circuit (pins 3/4) on another, never sharing a pair and never bonding mic ground to earphone ground. Earlier PinRef revisions twisted mic hot with the EARPHONE return and left mic ground alone on pin 4 \u2014 the worst pairing for mic-to-earphone coupling and the opposite of the manufacturer's advice; cables built to that layout must be re-terminated at BOTH ends or kept as a labelled matched pair. Clear-Com limits headset extensions to 15 ft because of capacitive coupling between mic and earphone lines; with Cat5 pairs keep it short and use shielded Cat5 with the drain on the XLR SHELL at the beltpack end only (not pin 1, not pin 3). Note that ordinary shielded Cat5 (F/UTP) has one overall screen \u2014 it is NOT the individually shielded mic pair Clear-Com requires \u2014 so treat 15 ft (5 m) TOTAL as the limit for the whole run, or use S/FTP cable. PinRef convention \u2014 no intercom manufacturer publishes a headset-over-RJ45 pinout (the amateur-radio OHIS layout exists and is incompatible: 5 V on pin 1, PTT on pin 2); both ends must be built from these two builds. Join the two ends only with a STRAIGHT-THROUGH T568B patch cable \u2014 a crossover swaps 1\u21943 and 2\u21946 and drives the pack's earphone output (and RS-700 15 V DC+) into the headset mic. The pack's mic bias (and on Clear-Com RS-700 packs ~15 V DC between pin 4 and pin 1) rides down the run. Pair with XLR4 \u2192 RJ45 at the beltpack end. A MALE XLR4 also fits 12 V camera-power outlets (pin 1 = 0 V, pin 4 = +12 V) \u2014 never plug this end into XLR4 power. Never plug the RJ45 into an Ethernet switch, a PoE port (44\u201357 V on pins 1/2 & 3/6 in Mode A, or 4/5 & 7/8 in Mode B/passive), a powered StudioHub+ panel (\u00b115 V on 7/8, DC GND on 4) ANY intercom RJ45 / Cat5-distributed party line (HelixNet powerline \u2248 \u00b130 V DC on pins 3\u20138), a Cat5 audio snake box (Catapult, Catdusa, RAT \u2014 48 V phantom on the pairs) or an OHIS headset jack \u2014 that would put up to 60 V (HelixNet \u00b130 V rails) or 44\u201357 V (PoE) across the headset mic and earphones. Label both RJ45 plugs HEADSET ONLY. Through the mated XLR4\u2192RJ45 build a 12 V outlet here would drive +12 V down RJ45 pin 3 into the beltpack's earphone output / RS-700 DC+ (XLR4 pin 4). Face/solder: the app draws the FEMALE XLR4 face. Clear-Com (MALE) variant of this end: that drawing is exactly what the male insert's solder cups look like \u2014 wire it straight from the FACE VIEW, no mirror. RTS PH-88R (FEMALE) variant: use the SOLDER SIDE view (the mirror). Confirm with the moulded pin numbers either way.",
