    notes: "Use this when handshake lines matter (some PCs/terminal emulators won't open the port without DTR/DSR). For most sports-timing use (OES, Fairplay, DVSport replay ingest; Swiss Timing / Omega units need special cables \u2014 Hy-Tek), the simpler 3-wire null modem is preferred \u2014 except Daktronics All Sport J5, which takes a STRAIGHT-THROUGH 9-pin (Daktronics KB DD2557298) \u2014 scoreboard controllers broadcast one-way, no flow control needed. DCD (pin 1) is left open. Commercial null modems vary: many tie the far end's DTR to DSR and DCD (4 \u2192 6 + 1, Wikipedia's full-handshake diagram), while StarTech's SCNM9FF passes DCD and RI straight (1-1, 9-9) \u2014 add the 4 \u2192 6 + 1 jumper inside each shell if software waits for carrier. Two-DTE null modems are normally female-female. Face/solder: the app draws the MALE DE-9 face. A FEMALE shell's solder cups are laid out exactly like that drawing, so wire a female shell straight from the FACE VIEW (its own front is the mirror, 1\u21945, 2\u21944, 6\u21949, 7\u21948, and the app's SOLDER SIDE view shows a MALE shell's cups). Confirm with the moulded numbers.",
