    notes: "AES59-2012 / Tascam DA-88 standard. Yamaha DB25 (AES3 digital) is NOT compatible \u2014 verify which standard the snake follows. Pair with XLR3\u2192DB25 at the source. Orientation: \u2018Ch1 at the far right\u2019 is true looking at the MATING FACE of the male plug (pin 1 top-left). Tascam's own drawing is the opposite hand (pin 13 top-left, channels 1\u20138 left to right) \u2014 that is the view you have of a male plug's SOLDER cups. Count pins by the moulded numbers, not by position. Phantom: console phantom on these XLR3s travels up every pair into the source device's line outputs \u2014 keep 48 V OFF on inputs fed from a line-level snake. The XLR3 tails are outputs, so by convention they are MALE (the app draws the female face). The male tails also fit party-line jacks \u2014 Clear-Com/RTS put +30 V DC on pin 2, which would land on the source device's output. Label the tails and keep them out of intercom runs.",
    sources: [
      { label: "TASCAM DB-25 Pinout Standard (official)", url: "https://www.tascam.eu/en/docs/DB-25_Pinout.pdf" },
      { label: "Aviom DB25 Pinout Information", url: "https://www.aviom.com/library/Technical-Resources/75_DB25-Pinout-Information.pdf" },
      { label: "AES59-2012 Standard", url: "https://www.aes.org/publications/standards/search.cfm?docID=92" },
      { label: "Yamaha \u2014 Phantom Power (never apply phantom to anything other than condenser microphones)", url: "https://hub.yamaha.com/proaudio/livesound/tools-of-the-trade-mixer-and-phantom-power/" },
      { label: "Clear-Com Solution Finder \u2014 3-pin XLR party-line pinout (1 Ground, 2 Voltage, 3 Audio)", url: "https://clear-com.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SF/pages/158784598" },
    ],
