    notes: "\u26a0\ufe0f PASSIVE SPEAKER USE ONLY. Speaker-level signal is tens of volts at high current \u2014 NEVER plug this into a mic input, line input, or powered speaker's input jack. Will destroy the input stage. Pin map per Neutrik drawing ST-NA4FX-M: 1+ (red) \u2192 XLR pin 2, 1\u2212 (black) \u2192 XLR pin 1, pin 3 open. Other XLR speaker conventions exist in the field: 2+/3\u2212 (silent through this adapter) and 1+/2\u2212 (polarity-inverted) \u2014 check the cabinet's internal wiring. An amp socket in Neutrik bridged mono (speaker across 1+/2+) feeds this 1+/1\u2212 adapter only the left-channel half at reduced level \u2014 do not \u2018fix\u2019 it by moving 1\u2212 to 2+. Genders: the XLR end of a Speakon-to-XLR speaker lead is MALE (Neutrik NA4FC-M/NA4FX-M; the app draws the female face). Do NOT bond the XLR shell to pin 1: on bridged/BTL (most Class-D) amps the 1\u2212 terminal is a live output, not ground. The male XLR3 also fits a party-line jack (+30 V DC on pin 2 into the amp output) every mic/line input on the stage, and 3-pin DMX outputs/thrus (speaker level destroys a DMX transceiver). Face/solder: the app draws the FEMALE mating face. For a MALE cable end that drawing is exactly what the male insert's solder cups look like, so wire the male straight from the FACE VIEW. For a FEMALE cable end use the app's SOLDER SIDE view (the mirror). Check the cabinet too: if its XLR pin 1 is bonded to the jack plate or chassis, a bridged/BTL amp puts the 1\u2212 output voltage on that metalwork. speakON has no rated breaking capacity: mute or power down the amp before mating or unmating under drive.",
