    notes: "Cable Wholesale black shell. Male DE-9. Wires cross in the middle \u2014 Yellow/Black swap, Green/Red swap. DE-9 side matches the published Telex KP98-7/KP32 matrix line connector (1 Data+, 2 Data\u2212, 4/5 station output = talk / TO MTX, 8/7 station input = listen / FROM MTX). The RJ45 pair assignment is this rig\u2019s own: the Clear-Com/ASG \u2018Adam DB9 to RJ45\u2019 drawing puts listen on the blue pair (4/5) and talk on the green pair (3/6) \u2014 the opposite of this shell \u2014 so label the shells and never mix the two conventions on one run. Against RTS's own keypanel RJ-45 (KP-5032 J8: 1 RS485+, 2 RS485\u2212, 3 FROM MTX+, 4 TO MTX+, 5 TO MTX\u2212, 6 FROM MTX\u2212, 7/8 RS485) this shell matches on pins 1, 2, 3 and 6 and has the TO MATRIX (talk) pair POLARITY INVERTED (shell: 5 = +, 4 = \u2212; RTS: 4 = +, 5 = \u2212). It does not match the XCP-48-RJ45 breakout (data on 2/7) or the Clear-Com/ASG drawing. The DE-9 face in the app shows RS-232 pin names \u2014 ignore them here; the wiring descriptions carry the keypanel signals. No ground/shield is carried (DE-9 3, 6, 9 open) \u2014 fine on short runs. Never plug the RJ45 into an Ethernet/PoE port or a HelixNet powerline jack.",
