    notes: "\u26a0\ufe0f UNVERIFIED \u2014 as recorded this is not a valid RS-422 null. Relative to the grey shell it moves only DE-9 pins 2 and 3 (RJ45 3\u21944), i.e. single legs of two different pairs: through a grey shell at the far end, TX\u2212 meets TX\u2212 (two drivers tied together), RX+ meets RX+, and pins 2/3 cross with inverted polarity, so no data can pass. A real RS-422 crossover swaps whole pairs with polarity kept \u2014 at this end: DE-9 2 (RX\u2212) \u2192 RJ45 6, 7 (RX+) \u2192 RJ45 3, 3 (TX+) \u2192 RJ45 5, 8 (TX\u2212) \u2192 RJ45 4 (then, via a grey 79 at the far end: 2\u21948, 7\u21943, 3\u21947, 8\u21942). Alternatively the Kalypso manual says RX/TX on pins 2&7 / 3&8 can be reversed in software, which would retire this shell. If this record describes a shell that is in service and works, the far-end device is not a 207M-style port \u2014 document which. Old note said \u2018pins 2\u21943 on the RJ45 side\u2019; the RJ45 pins that move are 3 and 4. Never plug the RJ45 into an Ethernet/PoE port or a HelixNet powerline jack (\u00b130 V on RJ45 3\u20138 would sit across the RS-422 driver and receiver pins). Kalypso end: Serial 1\u20138 / CPL (RS-422) ONLY \u2014 never the Diagnostic D-9, whose RS-232 pins 2 and 7 (\u00b112 V) would land on the far RS-422 transceiver (absolute maximum \u221210 V).",
